derivations.claim:
The flatness of the one-dimensional Fourier transform. For $1\le
u\le m$, differentiating $\widehat\phi(\xi)=\int e^{-2\pi i\xi x}\phi(x)\,dx$ under the integral sign and using the polynomial identity $\Delta_h^m(x^{
u-1})=0$ (the $m$-th finite difference of a polynomial of degree $
u-1\le m-1$ vanishes) gives $$\widehat\phi^{(
u)}(0)=(-2\pi i)^
u\int_{\mathbb R}x^{
u-1}\Delta_h^m\Theta(x)\,dx=(-2\pi i)^
u\int_{\mathbb R} igl(\Delta_h^mx^{
u-1} igr)(x)\,\Theta(x)\,dx=0,$$ where the middle equality is the self-adjointness $\int f\,(\Delta_h^m g)=\int(\Delta_h^mf)\,g$ of the even-order finite difference, which follows from the translation invariance of Lebesgue measure and $(\Delta_h)^*=-\Delta_h$.
